course: french
lesson: 31
title: Songs and rhymes
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: French children grow up with old songs that nobody owns any more - songs so old that the people who made them are long forgotten, so anyone may sing them anywhere.
explain: Songs are a clever way to learn: the tune carries the words into your memory, and many songs secretly teach numbers, body parts, clothes or how adjectives agree.
explain: Picture a French classroom on a rainy afternoon: half the class sings "Frère Jacques, frère Jacques, dormez-vous ?" and the other half starts a moment later, so the song chases itself round the room as a round.
explain: Some songs are sillier or darker than they sound: "Alouette, gentille alouette" is a cheerful tune about plucking a lark's feathers, one body part at a time.
explain: Others are games: in "Promenons-nous dans les bois" one child is the wolf, getting dressed piece by piece, until he shouts "J'arrive !" and chases everyone.
fact: Frère Jacques is about a monk who has overslept: dormez-vous ? means "are you sleeping?" and sonnez les matines means "ring the bells for morning prayers"; in English the bells are already ringing.
fact: Alouette means a lark; je te plumerai means "I will pluck your feathers", and each verse adds a body part: la tête, le bec, le cou, le dos.
fact: Sur le pont d'Avignon: le pont is the bridge, on y danse means "people dance there" and tous en rond means "all in a circle"; the real bridge has only four arches left and stops in the middle of the river.
fact: Many people say dancers really danced sous le pont, under the bridge, on an island in the river.
fact: Au clair de la lune means "in the moonlight"; Pierrot is a sad clown with a white face, and prête-moi ta plume means "lend me your pen", an old feather pen.
fact: Une souris verte is a silly rhyme about a green mouse running in the grass; it says verte, not vert, because souris is feminine.
fact: In Promenons-nous dans les bois the wolf answers "Loup, y es-tu ?" with clothes he is putting on: ma chemise, mon pantalon, mes chaussettes.
fact: Un, deux, trois, nous irons au bois is a counting rhyme where each number rhymes with the next line: trois and bois, six and cerises, douze and rouges.
fact: Savez-vous planter les choux ? asks if you know how to plant cabbages, using a different body part in each verse: le doigt, le pied, le genou, le coude, le nez.
fact: Il pleut, il pleut, bergère is a song from 1780: "It's raining, shepherdess, bring in your white sheep" - white sheep is blancs moutons.
fact: Meunier, tu dors means "Miller, you are sleeping"; children sing it slower and then faster, like the mill wheel.
fact: Ainsi font les petites marionnettes is a puppet song sung with twirling hands; p'tits is how petits is said in songs, but in writing you use petits.
exercise: What is Frère Jacques about? | A monk who has overslept | A boy who lost his dog | A king who could not sleep | A bell that has broken
exercise: What does dormez-vous ? mean? | Are you sleeping? | Are you dreaming? | Are you ringing? | Are you singing?
exercise: In "Alouette", what is an alouette? | A lark | A small almond cake | A French girl's name | A kind of guitar
exercise: What does je te plumerai mean? | I will pluck your feathers | I will write with your pen | I will sing you a song | I will feed you some plums
exercise: What does on y danse mean in "Sur le pont d'Avignon"? | People dance there | People sing there | People cross there | People fish there
exercise: What is special about the real bridge in Avignon? | It stops halfway across the river | It is the longest bridge in France | It is made all of glass | It is painted blue, white and red
exercise: Leo asks if Pierrot is a parrot. Who is he? | A sad clown with a white face | A green parrot who can talk | A king who lived on the Moon | A baker who sells bread at night
exercise: In "Au clair de la lune", what is a plume? | A pen | A plum to eat | A feather pillow for sleeping | A candle for the night
exercise: Why is it une souris verte, not vert? | Because souris is feminine | Because the mouse is very green | Because it rhymes with herbe | Because the song is plural
exercise: In "Promenons-nous dans les bois", what does the wolf answer? | What clothes he is putting on | How many children he can see | What he ate for breakfast | Where his house is
exercise: Which number rhymes with bois in the counting rhyme? | trois | six | neuf | douze
exercise: What do you plant in "Savez-vous planter les choux ?" | Cabbages | Carrots | Cherries | Flowers
exercise: In "Savez-vous planter les choux ?", what is le genou? | The knee | The elbow | The finger | The nose
exercise: In "Il pleut, il pleut, bergère", what is a bergère? | A shepherdess | A sheep | A farmer's dog | A rainy cloud
exercise: How do children sing "Meunier, tu dors"? | Slower and then faster, like the mill | Always very slowly, like a lullaby | In a whisper, so the miller sleeps | Only on the day of the harvest
exercise: In schoolwork, how do you write the p'tits of the puppet song? | petits | p'tits | ptits | petis
test: Your mate goes, "Frère Jacques is the same song in French and English, word for word, innit." Is he right? | No: in French he must ring the bells | Yes: every word is the same in both songs | No: in French the song is about a dog | Yes: but the French one is sung faster
test: Ruby from Belfast thinks "Alouette" is a soppy love song for a bird. Her teacher explains the words. Why does Ruby go "Ach, that's awful"? | It's about plucking the bird | The bird flies away and never comes back | The bird is cooked in the very first line | The song says birds are ugly and stupid
test: Gran from Bristol shows a photo of the broken bridge and goes, "Proper job, dancing on a broken bridge, my lover?" What do many people say? | They likely danced under it | People still dance on the bridge every night | The bridge broke because too many danced | Nobody ever danced, the song is about fishing
test: Jake from Texas sings "Savez-vous planter les choux ?" and goes, "Y'all, this song is unhinged - I'm planting with my nose!" Why is it a good song for school? | Each verse names a body part, so you learn them | It teaches you real gardening tips | It is the French national anthem | It helps you learn the days of the week
test: A cartoon shows "Une souris vert". Your sister says it looks fine. Fix it. | Une souris verte | Un souris vert | Une souris vertes | Un souris verte
test: Max from Newcastle plays the wolf game and goes, "Howay, I'm off!" as soon as the wolf says "Je mets ma chemise !" What's the problem? | He runs too early, before the wolf shouts J'arrive ! | Nothing, you run on the first clothes | He should run when the wolf says bonjour | The wolf never chases anyone
test: What would happen if you sang "Meunier, tu dors" at one speed all the way through? | You'd miss the fun of speeding up like the mill | The song would turn into Frère Jacques | Children would think the miller woke up | Nothing, it is always sung at one speed
test: Lily from Cardiff asks, "Un, deux, trois, nous irons au bois - is it just counting? Tidy." What's more to it? | The numbers rhyme with a story | It's just numbers with no other words | It's a song about a wolf in the woods | It counts sheep at bedtime
test: ★ Your cousin reads "blancs moutons" and goes, "Blank sheep? Like, empty?" What does it mean? | White sheep | Empty sheep | Black sheep | Small sheep
test: Kai from Auckland says, "Chur, why are these old songs free for everyone to sing?" What's the reason? | They are so old that nobody owns them any more | Their singers are all still paid by the school | They were made by a French teacher last year | They are free only in France
test: ★★★ Which song teaches clothes words, which teaches body parts, and which teaches numbers? | Promenons-nous, Alouette, Un deux trois | Alouette, Promenons-nous, Un deux trois | Un deux trois, Alouette, Promenons-nous | Promenons-nous, Un deux trois, Alouette
test: ★★★ Verses of "Alouette" add la tête, le bec, les yeux and le cou. How many of these four are masculine singular? | Two: le bec and le cou | One: only le bec | Three: le bec, le cou and les yeux | None: all four are feminine
exam: Identify the subject of the traditional French song "Frère Jacques". | A monk who has failed to wake in time | A shepherd in search of his sheep | A miller whose mill has ceased to turn | A clown in need of writing materials
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the expression "prête-moi ta plume" in the song "Au clair de la lune". | Lend me your pen | Give me your feather pillow | Pass me your plum | Show me your hat
exam: Select the reason for which the adjective in "une souris verte" bears a final e. | The noun souris is feminine | The noun souris is plural | The mouse is very green | The adjective precedes the noun
exam: Determine the date of composition of the song "Il pleut, il pleut, bergère". | 1780 | 1980 | 1880 | 1680
